.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH choom-view 1  "choom-view " 
.SH NAME
choom\-view \- Attach to a shared session as a viewer (a convenience wrapper over `ssh`)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBchoom\-view\fR [\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-\-server\fR] [\fB\-p\fR|\fB\-\-port\fR] [\fB\-\-ssh\-path\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fITARGET\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Attach to a shared session as a viewer (a convenience wrapper over `ssh`)
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-server\fR \fI<SERVER>\fR
Relay hostname when the target is a bare token [default: choom.sh]
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBCHOOM_SERVER\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-port\fR \fI<PORT>\fR
Relay SSH port [default: 22 for choom.sh, else 2200]
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBCHOOM_PORT\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-ssh\-path\fR \fI<SSH_PATH>\fR
Path to the `ssh` binary to exec [default: ssh on PATH]
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fITARGET\fR>
The share token, or `<token>@<host>`
.SH EXTRA
EXAMPLES:
  Attach with a token (host from \-s/default):
      choom view <token>
  Attach with a full token@host:
      choom view <token>@relay.example.com

`choom view` is a convenience over plain `ssh`: it adds the right \-p and runs the
real ssh binary (sidestepping wrappers like kitty\*(Aqs ssh kitten). The token decides
read\-only vs read\-write — stock `ssh <token>@host` works just as well.
